/*!

@page Display


# Display :

Following are the major features supported by QMMF-SDK Display:
- Support UI/Video layer Creation
- Dequeue and queue of layers from client to Display service
- Support for controlling various display states and parameters
- Create up to 4 surfaces (3RGB, 1 YUV), without Rotation
- Rotation, Brightness, Suspend Resume of Display
- Supports both Client allocated buffers and Display can allocate its own buffers
- Supports only hardware(MDP) Composition, no GPU composition on 8053 target
- Supports a display_panel for resolution of max 2.7K on 8053 target
.

\n

 @tableofcontents
______

@section Display_Diagrams Display Class Diagram
\n
@image html /Images/Display_Class_Diagram.png width=1200px

\n\n
______

@section Display_Buffer_Mgmt Buffer Management

We can have 2 modes of buffer allocation in Display
- Use Buffer (Buffers allocated by Client to Display)
- Allocate Buffer (Buffers are allocated by the Display Service)
.

<BLOCKQUOTE>
<b> use_buffer </b> flag of surfaceConfig is used to distinguish
between these two modes of buffer allocation.

\code{.cpp}
typedef struct SurfaceConfig {
  uint32_t width;
  uint32_t height;
  SurfaceFormat format;
  uint32_t buffer_count; // Number of buffers to be allocated/used
  bool cache; // To allocate cached or uncached buffers.
  bool use_buffer; // The client will allocate the buffers or display adapter
  SurfaceTransform surface_transform;
  uint32_t z_order;
} SurfaceConfig;
\endcode
</BLOCKQUOTE>

<BLOCKQUOTE>
The SurfaceConfig params are passed as an argument during CreateSurface.
- If the flag is set to 1, means Use Buffer Mode
- If the flag is set to 0, means Allocate Buffer Mode
.

</BLOCKQUOTE>

@subsection Use_Buffer_Mode Use Buffer Mode

- Create Surface by setting the <b> use_buffer </b> flag to 1.
\code{.cpp}
SurfaceConfig surface_config;
uint32_t surface_id;
memset(&surface_config, 0x0, sizeof surface_config);
surface_config.width = 1920;
surface_config.height = 1080;
surface_config.format = SurfaceFormat::kFormatYCbCr420SemiPlanarVenus;
surface_config.buffer_count = 4;
surface_config.cache = 0;
// use_buffer is 1 for Use Buffer Mode
surface_config.use_buffer = 1;
surface_config.z_order = 1;

ret = display_->CreateSurface(surface_config, &surface_id);
\endcode

- Display Service checks the use_buffer set to 1 and hence no buffers
will be allocated by the service.

- Client allocates the buffer by using Buffer Allocator.

- Since Client already has the buffer so, QueueSurfaceBuffer is called
instead of DequeueSurfaceBuffer.

@subsection Allocate_Buffer_Mode Allocate Buffer Mode

- Client sets the use_buffer flag to 0 for Allocate Buffer Mode
- Buffers are allocated by the Display Service as a part of CreateSurface API
- Client need to call DequeueSurfaceBuffer to get the empty buffer allocated
by the service, before it can call QueueSurfaceBuffer
.
\n

______

@section Display_Buffer_States Buffer States

There are mainly 4 buffer states used by Display:
- kStateFree : The buffer is free and can be used by client.
- kStateDequeued : The buffer is being used by client to fill the data for rendering.
- kStateQueued : The buffer is ready for composition and display.
- kStateCommitted : The buffer is being used by display for rendering.
.

Following section explains how Buffer States are changes during different Buffer Allocation Modes.

@image html /Images/Display_Buffer_State.png width=800px
\n

@subsection bs_Use_Buffer_Mode Use Buffer Mode

- Buffer is allocated by the Display Service and Buffer State is set to <b> kStateFree </b>
- Client calls DequeueSurfaceBuffer to get the free buffer, the buffer state is changed to
<b> kStateDequeued </b> as a part of DequeuSurfaceBuffer.
- From <b> kStateDequeued </b>, following state change can happen:
 - The buffer state can be changed to <b> kStateFree </b>, if the display went to Suspended state
 - The buffer state is changed to <b> kStateQueued </b>, when QueueSurfaceBuffer is called.
- If Display is getting the buffers at higher rate compared to the refresh rate, then the
<b> kStateQueued </b> buffers are discarded.
\n
Generally the refresh rate for Display is 60 Hz i.e. every 16.66 ms. If the fps is high, let's say 120,
then we will be getting buffers every 8.33 ms, so discard the already queued buffers.
\n
From <b> kStateQueued </b>, following state change can happen:
 - The buffer state of older queued buffer is changed to <b> kStateFree </b> if new <b> kStateQueued </b>
 buffer came.
 - The buffer can move to <b> kStateCommitted </b>. In this case the already <b> kStateCommitted </b> buffer state
 is changed to <b> kStateFree </b>. This step happens as a part of GetLayerStack API at every 16.66 ms (refresh rate).
- Once a buffer comes to <b> kStateCommitted </b> state, it is used by the Display for rendering.
From <b> kStateCommitted </b> state, buffer change it's state to <b> kStateFree </b>, only if there
is a <b> kStateQueued </b> buffer available whose state can be changed to <b> kStateCommitted </b>
This step happens as a part of GetLayerStack API at every 16.66 ms (refresh rate)
.

@subsection bs_Allocate_Buffer_Mode Allocate Buffer Mode

- Client directly call QueueSurfaceBuffer API, as it already has the buffer.
The buffer state is changed set to <b> kStateQueued </b> as a part of QueueSurfaceBuffer API.
- Other Buffer State transition is same as Use Buffer Mode
.
\n

______

@section Display_Debug_Knobs Debug Knobs

- The following setprop can be used to enable Display Logging of sdm:
  - <b> adb shell setprop persist.qmmf.display.log 1 </b>
- The following setprop can be used to enable qmmf-sdk logs
  <b> adb shell setprop persist.qmmf.sdk.log.level </b> with following values
  - 0, Info/Error/Warning
  - 1, Debug logs are enabled
  - 2, Verbose logs are enabled
- The Prepare time and Commit time should be less than 16.66ms for refresh rate of 60Hz
- The ion usage for a particular usecase can be dumped to a file using following:
  - <b> cat /sys/kernel/debug/ion/heaps/system > dump_usage.txt </b>
.
\n

______

@section Display_Rotation Display Rotation

We can rotate the Display with following 4 values with the help of SurfaceTransform.
- Rotate by 0 degree
- Rotate by 90 degrees
- Rotate by 180 degree
- Rotate by 270 degree
.

We can define the Transform params at CreateSurface. The params are also checked at HandleVSync event for the queued buffer.
<BLOCKQUOTE>
The following structure defines rotation and flip values for a display layer.
\code{.cpp}
typedef struct SurfaceTransform {
  float rotation; // rotate the layer based on left most pixel coordinate
  bool flip_horizontal; // Mirror reversal of the layer across a horizontal axis
  bool flip_vertical; // Mirror reversal of the layer across a vertical axis
} SurfaceTransform;
\endcode
</BLOCKQUOTE>

<BLOCKQUOTE>
Followings are the SurfaceTransform flag values for different kind of Rotations.
- 0 degree
\code{.cpp}
surface_param.surface_transform.rotation = 0.0f;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_horizontal = 0;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_vertical = 0;
\endcode
- 90 degrees
\code{.cpp}
surface_param.surface_transform.rotation = 90.0f;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_horizontal = 0;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_vertical = 0;
\endcode
- 180 degrees
\code{.cpp}
surface_param.surface_transform.rotation = 0.0f;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_horizontal = 1;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_vertical = 1;
\endcode
- 270 degrees
\code{.cpp}
surface_param.surface_transform.rotation = 90.0f;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_horizontal = 1;
surface_param.surface_transform.flip_vertical = 1;
\endcode
.
</BLOCKQUOTE>
\n

______

@section Display_Scaling Display Scaling

The Display can be scaled from a src Resolution to a dest Resolution with the help of SurfaceParam

<BLOCKQUOTE>
\code{.cpp}
typedef struct SurfaceParam {
  SurfaceTransform surface_transform;
  SurfaceRect src_rect; // Rectangular area of the buffer to consider for composition
  SurfaceRect dst_rect; // The target position where the frame will be displayed
  SurfaceBlending surface_blending;
  SurfaceFlags surface_flags;
  uint8_t plane_alpha; // Alpha value applied to the whole surface
  uint32_t frame_rate;
  uint32_t solid_fill_color;
} SurfaceParam;
\endcode

The Rectangular Area defined by SurfaceRect for src and dest rectangles is defined by following struct
\code{.cpp}
typedef struct SurfaceRect {
  float left; // leftmost pixel position
  float top; // top pixel position
  float right; // right pixel position
  float bottom; // bottom pixel position
} SurfaceRect;
\endcode
</BLOCKQUOTE>

<BLOCKQUOTE>
Following is the snippet for scaling a 1080p area to 480p
\code{.cpp}
memset(&surface_param, 0x0, sizeof surface_param);

surface_param.src_rect = {0.0, 0.0, 1920.0, 1080.0};
surface_param.dst_rect = {0.0, 0.0, 480.0, 360.0};
// other params

display_->QueueSurfaceBuffer(surface_id, surface_buffer, surface_param);
\endcode
</BLOCKQUOTE>
\n

______

*/
